	// sessions_revoked reports whether the post-reset sweep actually ran.
	//
	// A reset revokes every existing session and API token, because a reset is
	// what somebody does when they believe a credential is compromised. That
	// sweep is deliberately best-effort — a failure must not undo the password
	// change, which has already happened and cannot be rolled back by then.
	//
	// What was missing is that nobody was TOLD. The error was discarded at the
	// call site under a comment claiming "logged by the caller's interceptor",
	// and the plugin has no logging at all: grep handlers/ for slog, log or
	// logger and there is nothing. So a reset after a theft could leave the
	// thief's session alive, report success, and end there (marb #58).
	//
	// `false` means: your password IS changed, and the old sessions may still
	// be live — sign out everywhere, or reset again. A caller that ignores this
	// field is no worse off than before; one that reads it can say the true
